MergeTeX -- Run Macaulay2 code inside a LaTeX file
**************************************************

Description
===========

This packages parses a LaTeX file, looking for Macaulay2 code. The latter must
follow the syntax of the LaTeX package "listings", namely, if must be enclosed
in \begin{lstlisting}[language=Macaulay2]...\end{lstlisting}, or must be taken
from an external M2 file via
\begin{lstinputlisting}[language=Macaulay2]{filename.m2}; one can also insert
inline code with \lstinline[language=Macaulay2]!...!, but it is not parsed. See
the example file ex.tex.

It then produces a new file where the output of the code has been inserted in
place of the input. The LaTeX file should contain \usepackage{listings} and
should include a definition of the Macaulay2 language; this can be found in the
auxiliary file lst-Macaulay2.tex.

It also replaces automatically commands of the form \macoutput{n} with the nth
output of Macaulay2.

mergeTeX takes as input the TeX source as a string and returns the output as a
string, whereas mergeTeXFile takes as arguments the names of the source LaTeX
file and of the target LaTeX file (the two can be identical; mergeTeXFile acts
idempotently).

